session:pr439Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Dag asked to help run dagalog and the backlog dashboard from this server safely, with Google OAuth login for his personal account. Investigation of sparql_endpoint/src/auth.rs found the existing generic OIDC tier authorizes by role claim (read_role/write_role/admin_role), but a personal @gmail.com ID token carries no roles claim at all -- that's an Entra ID/Google Workspace concept -- so every request would be denied (fails closed, but useless for actual login). Also found the browser sign-in UI wired to browser_client_id in frontend.html only loads MSAL.js (Microsoft's library); the doc comment describing it as covering Google Identity Services too is aspirational, not implemented. This server also turned out to be a Hetzner cloud VM with a public IP and no domain/DNS/TLS setup at all, which is a hard blocker for Google OAuth specifically (Google rejects bare-IP origins for anything but localhost). Asked Dag two clarifying questions (network exposure level, auth architecture) before touching anything, since this is a real security decision on an internet-reachable box, not something to guess at. His answers: fully open to the internet is fine, and he wants to plan for future fine-grained per-graph/per-triple access control, which he assumed meant building OAuth into dagalog itself. Filed that as its own epic (#437) rather than conflating it with this deployment task -- data-level ACL is substantial standalone engine work, not an auth-tier detail. For the actual login gate, built edge-proxy config templates (Caddy + oauth2-proxy) instead of in-app dagalog OIDC support, since in-app would mean writing new security-critical Rust code plus TWO separate from-scratch Google Identity Services browser integrations (frontend.html and backlog_frontend.html are separate origins per #381's process split) immediately exposed to the public internet, versus zero new app code with an edge gate using battle-tested software that covers both services uniformly. Confirmed with Dag mid-conversation this doesn't foreclose in-app OIDC later for #437's finer-grained needs. Could not live-test any of this since no domain exists yet for the server -- documented that limitation explicitly in PUBLIC_DEPLOYMENT.md along with a deny-path verification checklist to run before trusting any real deployment." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:439 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr439Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr439 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr439Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Access control for who gets through the Google sign-in gate is an explicit oauth2-proxy AUTHENTICATED_EMAILS_FILE allowlist (one email per line, gitignored), not Google Workspace domain restriction or OAuth consent-screen scoping." ;
    agp:alternative "Relying on Google Workspace's own domain-restricted OAuth consent screen (hd claim / organization-only apps). Rejected: this is a personal @gmail.com account, not a Google Workspace organization, so there is no domain to restrict to -- Workspace-level restriction isn't available for this use case at all, making the explicit email-allowlist file the only real option, not merely the preferred one." .
